#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: typed-pattern-transitive
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# A typed pattern matches through an intermediate trait

Regression for BUGS.md `typed-pattern-misses-a-subtype-reached-through-an-intermediate-trait`.
A typed pattern used to see only the case classes naming a type DIRECTLY, so one trait in
between and the match silently took the wrong branch — no refusal, no diagnostic. Both
fronts had it, and neither could be fixed alone: F is held byte-identical to the reference
lowerer, so a transitive expansion in one would have changed its emitted tag tests.

The case covers what the fix has to get right rather than only the headline:

  - `Dog` reaches `Being` through TWO traits, so one hop is not enough;
  - a direct child (`Snake extends Animal`) must still match, which is the byte-identity half;
  - a MIDDLE type (`Mammal`) must match its own child, not just the root;
  - a non-member (`7`) must still fall through — the widened predicate must not match everything.

NO CYCLE CASE HERE, and the reason is worth keeping. The ancestor walk is fuel-bounded (16)
rather than cycle-detecting, and a `trait A extends B` / `trait B extends A` pair was drafted
to prove it terminates. It does — on INT and JS. But the JVM lane compiles real Scala, and
**Scala rejects that program outright**: `Cyclic inheritance: trait A extends itself`. So the
input is not legal in the language and cannot be a conformance case; asserting behaviour on it
would be asserting behaviour on a program no user can write. The fuel bound stays as defence in
depth for a malformed hierarchy reaching the lowerer past the checker, and it is documented at
the walk itself in both fronts rather than pinned here.

```scalascript
trait Being
trait Animal extends Being
trait Mammal extends Animal
case class Dog(n: Int) extends Mammal
case class Snake(n: Int) extends Animal

def being(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: Being => "being"
  case _ => "other"

def mammal(x: Any): String = x match
  case _: Mammal => "mammal"
  case _ => "other"

println(being(Dog(1)))
println(being(Snake(2)))
println(being(7))
println(mammal(Dog(3)))
println(mammal(Snake(4)))
```
